WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.610
<v ->Okay, so one last service to know.</v>

2
00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:02.770
Going into the exam.

3
00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:03.620
At a very high level

4
00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.880
this is more the focus of the developer exam.

5
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.380
But AWS X-Ray allows you to get visual analysis

6
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.560
of your application

7
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.730
and get tracing capability.

8
00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.160
So you can see your clients setting data into

9
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.310
different microservices and what these services are doing.

10
00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.720
And then you get some information around the latency,

11
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.470
the error rates and you can follow and trace

12
00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.250
how your network calls are moving

13
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.430
across your infrastructure.

14
00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:28.960
So if you're having issues somewhere,

15
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.180
X-Ray is going to be great to figure out

16
00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.710
where the issue came from in the first place.

17
00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.280
So X-Ray is to trace requests across your microservices.

18
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.470
This is very helpful when you have a distributed system

19
00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.090
and many microservices.

20
00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.670
It has integrations with many, many things.

21
00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:45.300
The first one is EC2 instances,

22
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.840
and for this you need to install the X-Ray agent.

23
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.480
Also ECS, you need to install the X-Ray agent

24
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.740
or use a Docker container for X-Ray.

25
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.360
Lambda function, this is just a box you have to tick.

26
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.910
Beanstalk, the agent will be automatically installed

27
00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.980
if you enable the X-Ray integration.

28
00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:03.590
API Gateway, very helpful if you want to debug errors

29
00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.170
such as your 504 for your timeouts.

30
00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:10.020
And your X-Ray agents or your services that use X-Ray

31
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.440
need the right IAM permissions to talk to Amazon X-Ray.

32
00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.170
That's it, that's very simple.

33
00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.530
So any time you see distributed tracing

34
00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.380
and troubleshooting at the request level,

35
00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.230
think X-Ray, and that's all you need to know

36
00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:24.430
going into the exam.

37
00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.263
All right, that's it, I will see you in the next lecture.

